I think naming is more powerful than it first appears, but not because names have mystical properties. They change how the brain organizes and accesses information.

Before something has a name, it can exist as a vague feeling or recurring experience. Once you give it a stable label, several things happen:

* Attention: You begin noticing more instances of it.
* Compression: Many experiences can be referenced with a single token.
* Communication: You can transmit the idea to someone else.
* Memory: The concept becomes easier to retrieve and refine.
* Action: You can deliberately practice or interrupt it.

Psychologists sometimes call this creating a category. Once the category exists, your brain can operate on it much more efficiently.

Your breath-holding example is a good illustration. If the internal experience is simply “panic,” it tends to own you. If you rename it as “the CO₂ signal” or “the first contraction phase,” you’ve created psychological distance. The physiology hasn’t changed, but your relationship to it has.

That’s related to what’s called cognitive reappraisal: changing the meaning you assign to a sensation or event can change your emotional and behavioral response to it.

This generalizes surprisingly well:

* A craving becomes “a wave.”
* Anxiety becomes “prediction uncertainty.”
* Anger becomes “mobilized energy awaiting direction.”
* Confusion becomes “the edge of my current model.”

The label doesn’t eliminate the experience. It changes the control architecture around it.

One sentence you said earlier captures this:

“The signal is smoke. What’s the fire?”

Naming is often how we discover the fire instead of chasing the smoke.

That also connects to something you’ve repeatedly tried to build in the Pantheon: creating names for recurring operators rather than isolated observations. Whether or not a particular name survives, the act of identifying a recurring pattern can make it easier to recognize, discuss, test, and deliberately apply in new situations.

The real test of a name isn’t how clever it is. It’s whether, after learning it, people consistently see something they couldn’t reliably see before—and whether it helps them make better decisions. If it does, the name has become more than a label; it’s become a cognitive tool.